=begin pod

=head1 MCP::Server

A framework for building MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers in Raku.
Register tools, resources, and prompts — the framework handles the
JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol, initialization handshake, and message dispatch.

=head2 Synopsis

=begin code :lang<raku>
use MCP::Server;

my $server = MCP::Server.new(:name<my-tools>, :version<1.0>);

$server.tool: 'greet',
    description => 'Greet someone by name',
    params => {
        name => { type => 'string', description => 'Name to greet', required => True },
    },
    handler => -> :%args { "Hello, {%args<name>}!" };

$server.run;  # Listens on stdin/stdout
=end code

Use with Claude Code:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-tools": {
      "command": "raku",
      "args": ["-I", "lib", "examples/my-server.raku"]
    }
  }
}
=end code

=head2 Tools

Tools are functions the LLM can call. Define parameters with types and
descriptions — the framework generates JSON Schema automatically.

=begin code :lang<raku>
$server.tool: 'search',
    description => 'Search the web',
    params => {
        query => { type => 'string', description => 'Search query', required => True },
        limit => { type => 'integer', description => 'Max results' },
    },
    handler => -> :%args {
        # Return a string (wrapped as text content)
        "Results for {%args<query>}..."
    };
=end code

Tool handlers receive C<:%args> and return:
=item C<Str> — wrapped as C<[{type: "text", text: $result}]>
=item C<List> — passed through as content items
=item On exception — returned as C<isError: true> content

=head2 Resources

Resources expose data the LLM can read.

=begin code :lang<raku>
$server.resource: 'config://app',
    name => 'App Config',
    description => 'Application configuration',
    mime-type => 'application/json',
    handler => -> :%args { '{"debug": true}' };
=end code

=head2 Prompts

Prompts are pre-defined templates for the user.

=begin code :lang<raku>
$server.prompt: 'review',
    description => 'Code review prompt',
    arguments => [
        { name => 'code', description => 'Code to review', required => True },
    ],
    handler => -> :%args { "Please review:\n{%args<code>}" };
=end code

=head2 Examples

=item C<examples/echo-server.raku> — Minimal echo and reverse tools
=item C<examples/weather-server.raku> — Weather via wttr.in
=item C<examples/file-server.raku> — File system tools
=item C<examples/strawberry-server.raku> — The tool LLMs wish they had

=head2 Protocol

Implements MCP protocol version C<2025-11-25> over stdio:

=item JSON-RPC 2.0 message format
=item C<initialize> / C<initialized> handshake
=item C<tools/list>, C<tools/call>
=item C<resources/list>, C<resources/read>
=item C<prompts/list>, C<prompts/get>
=item C<ping>
=item Logging via C<notifications/message>
=item Full error handling with standard JSON-RPC error codes

=head2 Transport

Default transport is stdio (stdin/stdout). Custom transports can be
created by implementing the C<MCP::Server::Transport> role:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use MCP::Server::Transport;

class MyTransport does MCP::Server::Transport {
    method read-message(--> Str) { ... }
    method write-message(Str:D $msg) { ... }
}

$server.run(:transport(MyTransport.new));
=end code

